3 STAE Tasmania
3 CTRY Australia
1 OCCU Whaler, Merchant
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.fortysouth.com.au/coining-the-hedberg?amp=1
2 CONT
2 CONT [PIC]
2 CONT
2 CONT O.H. Hedberg penny, originally issued 1854. Image couresty of the Museums Victoria
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Hilmer Hedberg was a Swedish immigrant who arrived in Hobart in 1844 at the age of 27. He was an impressive man. He initially worked for wealthy Hobart ship owner, Charles Seal, and soon owned two whaling ships – the Victoria
2 CONC  and Maid of Erin – that provided oil processed and exported by nine separate businesses in Hobart around that time. When the bottom fell out of the whaling market Olaf established an oil and colour store at 2 Argyle St, mentioned
2 CONC  on the reverse of the coin. He offered the tokens, as many other tradespeople did, to overcome the chronic shortage of small change in the young colony. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course, the tokens were also a very effective means of advertising Olaf’s business. Newspaper advertisements for the store list an EXTENSIVE STOCK of CLARIFIED OILS, VIZ. — Black, at 2s 6d per gall, Black Fish at 3s 6d, Sperm a
2 CONC t 7s . . . . as well as a quantity of Fashionable bed-room LAMPS, LAMPSHADES, of various sizes, Ditto GLASSES 7 & CHIMNIES, of every description. Neat’s Foot Oil, Sweet Oil and Turpentine, Floating lights and Fireworks completed t
2 CONC he list. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf was also Superintendent of Hobart Fire Brigade for 25 years. A commemorative award for 8 years of service, framed ornately in gold, shows a grey-bearded gentleman with bow tie and fireman’s cap. His obituary in 1884 describes
2 CONC  him as filling the position with much proficiency and notes that he was very brave in extinguishing fires. A man of “Herculean strength”, he is reported to have carried trapped residents to safety and was praised for extinguishin
2 CONC g a fire next to his shop in 1852. This fire started in Mr Gillies’ premises when Mr Joynes was preparing a tarpaulin steeped in a solution of naptha and oil for export to Port Philip. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [PIC]
2 CONT
2 CONT A commendation presented to Olaf Hilmer Hedberg Snr by the Derwent and Tamar Fire Brigade. Note the photographs of Hedberg’s store in Argyle Street on upper left and right. Image courtesy of the Tasmanian Archives and Heritage Off
2 CONC ice.
2 CONT
2 CONT A report says it ignited and burned “with the most intense heat and fury, enveloping all around in the densest smoke”. Mr Hedberg’s solution was to demolish the adjoining weather board buildings with a grappling iron and chains, t
2 CONC hus arresting the progress of the fire. Hobart’s Courier praised his “promptitude and energy”.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later in the century Olaf raced yachts, such as the appropriately named Viking, in the Hobart Regatta. On his death in 1912 ((the son, the brewer)), the Mercury’s yachting notes describe him as a “leading light in the Yachting wor
2 CONC ld” and one who did a great deal towards improving the type of yachts on the Derwent. He built the Una, Vega, Gladys, Olga and Viking, as well as the Nordica that by “a peculiar coincidence” was wrecked on the day he died. Olaf’s 
2 CONC obituary makes particular note of this “much-respected” resident of Hobart who went to sea at an early age and served for a time on the missionary schooner Triton in the Pacific. It concludes with the comment that his smiling face
2 CONC  would be missed by many a friend. And, indeed, it is reported that all the boats in port had their flags half-masted on the days following his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Viking 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://m.facebook.com/pg/higgspodcasts2016on/posts/
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hedberg family’s link with ships and water races continued with Olaf Snr’s great, great, great grandson, Will Hedberg, crewing on the Brindabella in the 2013 Sydney Hobart Yacht Race. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, the fourth Olaf (Ollie) Hilmer Hedberg, who turned 80 this year, was Commodore of the Royal Yacht Club of Tasmania in the 1980s, chairman of the race committee for the memorable Tall Ships Parade at Australia’s Bicente
2 CONC nary celebrations and is a Life Member of the Club. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was chairman of Public Sector Mapping Agencies (PSMA) Australia for eleven years and has worked extensively for Natural Resource Management in Tasmania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2008 he was awarded an AM in the Australia Day Honours for services to Land Information Tasmania and the national spatial information industry.
2 CONT
