1 OCCU Professor, Kemist
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=18647
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torbern_Bergman
2 CONT
2 CONT Torbern Olaf (Olof) Bergman (KVO) (20 March 1735 – 8 July 1784) was a Swedish chemist and mineralogist noted for his 1775 Dissertation on Elective Attractions, containing the largest chemical affinity tables ever published. Bergma
2 CONC n was the first chemist to use the A, B, C, etc., system of notation for chemical species.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and education
2 CONT
2 CONT Torbern was born on 20 March 1735, the son of Barthold Bergman and Sara Hägg. He enrolled at the University of Uppsala at age 17. His father wished him to read either law or divinity, while he himself was anxious to study mathemat
2 CONC ics and natural science; in the effort to please both himself and his father, he overworked himself and harmed his health. During a period of enforced abstinence from study, he amused himself with field botany and entomology. He w
2 CONC as able to send Linnaeus specimens of several new kinds of insects, and in 1756 he succeeded in proving that, contrary to the opinion of that naturalist, the so-called Coccus aquaticus was really the ovum of a kind of leech. He re
2 CONC turned to the university in 1758, and received his Ph.D. in that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Bergman lectured at the university on physics and mathematics. Upon the resignation of the celebrated Wallerius, Bergman was a candidate for the professorship of chemistry and mineralogy. His competitors charged him with ignorance
2 CONC  of the subject, because he had never written on it. To refute them, he shut himself up for some time in a laboratory, and prepared a treatise on the manufacture of alum, which became a standard work. He was appointed a professor 
2 CONC of chemistry, and remained at this position for the rest of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bergman greatly contributed to the advancement of quantitative analysis, and he developed a mineral classification scheme based on chemical characteristics and appearance. He is noted for his research on the chemistry of metals, e
2 CONC specially bismuth and nickel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1764, Bergman was elected a member of the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. In April, 1765 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of London.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1771, four years after Joseph Priestley first created artificially carbonated water, Bergman invented a process to make carbonated water from chalk by the action of sulfuric acid. Finally he is noted for his sponsorship of Carl
2 CONC  Wilhelm Scheele, whom some deem to be Torbern's "greatest discovery".
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1771, Bergman married Margareta Catharina Trast.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT In Bergman's honor, the uranium mineral Torbernite and the lunar crater Bergman both bear his name.
